<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States Bill of Rights</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Bill of Rights is the first ten amendments to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>, proposed by Congress in 1789 and ratified in 1791. Drafted largely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> to answer Anti-Federalist fears of a strong central government, it guarantees freedom of religion, speech, the press and assembly, the right to bear arms and the rights of the accused, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-bill-of-rights.jpg" alt="United States Bill of Rights" width="320" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Archives and Records Administration. Office of the Federal Register. 4/1/1985 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bill_of_Rights_-_DPLA_-_a65f6d0b6210c18d17c596908ebcf270_(page_1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bill of Rights followed the often bitter debate of 1787 and 1788 over ratification of the Constitution. Anti-Federalists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrick-henry">Patrick Henry</a>, Samuel Adams and Richard Henry Lee objected that it contained no bill of rights, and Elbridge Gerry's objections went through 46 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printings</a>. Federalists largely opposed adding one. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a> argued in The Federalist No. 84 that the Constitution was itself a bill of rights, since the people surrendered nothing and so needed no particular reservations.</p><p>The Massachusetts convention broke the impasse when Samuel Adams and John Hancock agreed to ratification on condition that amendments also be proposed. Its proposals included a grand jury requirement and a reservation of powers to the states, which later shaped the Fifth and Tenth Amendments. James Madison first opposed a list of rights, fearing that anything left off it would seem unprotected, but he agreed to support one and became its author, including a Ninth Amendment to meet that fear. His sources included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a>, the English Bill of Rights of 1689 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia-declaration-of-rights">Virginia Declaration of Rights</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Congress approved twelve articles of amendment on 25 September 1789 and sent them to the states on 2 October. Articles Three to Twelve were ratified on 15 December 1791 and became the First to Tenth Amendments. Article Two became the Twenty-seventh Amendment on 5 May 1992, and Article One is still pending. They were added as supplements, not worked into the body of the Constitution as Madison had proposed.</p><p>The First Amendment bars laws respecting an establishment of religion or abridging speech, the press, assembly and petition. The Second protects the right to keep and bear arms, and the Third limits the quartering of soldiers in private homes. The Fourth to Eighth cover searches, grand juries, double jeopardy, self-incrimination, due process, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a>, bail and cruel and unusual punishment. The Ninth says the listing of certain rights does not deny others, and the Tenth reserves unlisted powers to the states or the people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The amendments applied only to the federal government, a limit affirmed in Barron v. Baltimore in 1833. The Bill had little judicial effect for about 150 years, and the Court made no important decision protecting free speech until 1931. After the Fourteenth Amendment was ratified in the 1860s, federal and state courts began from the early 20th century to apply parts of the Bill to state and local governments, a process called incorporation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> had fourteen handwritten copies made; the copy kept by the First Congress has been on display at the National Archives in Washington since 13 December 1952.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United States Bill of Rights” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its first ten amendments, the <b>Bill of Rights</b> of 1791, protected freedom of speech, religion and the press and the rights of the accused.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
